A Theoretical Framework for Adaptive Force Safety in Human–AI Assisted Intervention

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Version: 0.4 — Theoretical Dissertation Draft
Date: 2026-08-19
Domain: Artificial Intelligence, Control Theory, Public Safety, Human–AI Systems, Algorithmic Governance

---

Abstract

This manuscript presents a purely theoretical framework for preventing excessive, unnecessary, or insufficiently adaptive use of force during high-risk human interventions. It treats intervention not as a single legal or moral decision, but as a closed-loop control problem under uncertainty and adversarial disturbance.

The central hypothesis is that many catastrophic intervention outcomes are not solely failures of individual morality, institutional culture, or legal compliance. They are also failures of closed-loop control under uncertainty.

Version 0.4 is a theoretical manuscript. It contains no empirical results, no physical tests, no simulation outcomes, and no trained machine-learning models. All algorithmic components are specified formally but were not implemented, trained, or tested. The contribution is architectural and formal: it defines a runtime assurance structure in which intervention intensity is constrained by control barrier functions, monitored by an independent Safety Governor, subjected to proxy-bias auditing, and verified through an active human authority layer.

The framework is organized around five principles:

1. Threat-conditional intervention;
2. Minimum sufficient intervention within a hard safety envelope;
3. Dynamic de-escalation under bounded, auditable exceptions;
4. Physiological, environmental, and mobility safety as hard constraints;
5. Continuous human–AI reassessment with active human verification.

The proposed architecture includes multimodal perception, probabilistic state estimation, proxy-bias monitoring, risk assessment, constrained control, an independent Safety Governor, and a Cognitive Forcing Function for human oversight. The operational policy is identity-independent: protected characteristics are not operational inputs. Identity independence is treated as a continuously audited property, not an assumption.

The objective is not to automate coercive authority. It is to construct a human–AI safety architecture in which intervention intensity is mathematically constrained, continuously adapted to changing reality, and human authority is protected against both automation bias and algorithmic overreach.

