2. Related Foundations

This work draws on several established research areas.

2.1 Runtime Assurance and Safety Governors

The Action Governor and Black-Box Simplex Architecture provide formal methods for supervising an unverified advanced controller with a safety layer [1, 3]. In those frameworks, a baseline safe controller or a stored safe action sequence is used when the advanced controller would violate safety constraints.

The present framework adapts this idea to human–AI intervention systems. The operational controller may be a learned policy or a human recommendation; the Safety Governor enforces hard constraints independently.

2.2 Control Barrier Functions

Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) are used to enforce forward invariance of safe sets in safety-critical control [2, 6]. A set \mathcal{S} is forward invariant if a control law exists that keeps the system within \mathcal{S} for all future times.

In this framework, CBFs formalize physiological, environmental, and mobility safety as hard constraints. If a barrier approaches zero, the Safety Governor must prioritize safety over all operational objectives.

2.3 Use-of-Force Doctrine and De-escalation Evidence

Empirical policing research provides evidence that structured de-escalation training can reduce use-of-force incidents, citizen injuries, and officer injuries [4, 5, 10]. However, those results are external to this manuscript. They are cited only to motivate the framework, not as evidence for the framework’s validity.

2.4 Fairness and Bias in Algorithmic Systems

Fairness metrics such as equalized odds, disparate impact, and demographic parity are formal tools for auditing algorithmic bias [24, 25]. The present framework uses these metrics not because bias is assumed, but because identity independence must be continuously tested.

