4. State, Threat, Uncertainty, and Safety Representation

4.1 Threat as Multidimensional Observable State

Threat is represented as a vector of observable components:

\mathbf{T}_t = 
\begin{bmatrix}
T^{\text{weapon}}_t \\
T^{\text{proximity}}_t \\
T^{\text{motion}}_t \\
T^{\text{resistance}}_t \\
T^{\text{environment}}_t
\end{bmatrix}.

Each component is estimated independently and combined only when operationally necessary.

Intent is deliberately excluded. The estimation of unobservable mental states is epistemically unreliable, legally risky, and highly prone to bias. The framework uses only observable behavior, kinematics, and environmental indicators.

Force policy is therefore a function over this vector:

\pi_F = \pi_F(\mathbf{T}_t, \mathbf{U}_t, \Phi_t, E_t, M_t),

where:

· \mathbf{T}_t is the estimated threat vector;
· \mathbf{U}_t is the uncertainty estimate;
· \Phi_t is the physiological safety state;
· E_t is the environmental safety state;
· M_t is the mobility and positional safety state.

4.2 Uncertainty Quantification

Uncertainty is decomposed into epistemic and aleatoric components:

\mathbf{U}_t = \left( U^{epi}_t, U^{ale}_t \right).

Epistemic uncertainty U^{epi}_t is estimated using deep ensembles. Given K independently trained threat-estimation models g_k, each producing a threat vector estimate \mathbf{T}_t^{(k)}, define:

\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K} \mathbf{T}_t^{(k)},

U^{epi}_t = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\left\| \mathbf{T}_t^{(k)} - \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t \right\|^2.

Aleatoric uncertainty U^{ale}_t is estimated from sensor noise covariance:

U^{ale}_t = \mathrm{tr}\left(\Sigma_{v,t}\right),

where \Sigma_{v,t} is the estimated observation noise covariance.

Environmental adaptation is handled through polynomial chaos expansion, allowing the perception layer to adapt to weather, lighting, sensor degradation, and other environmental shifts without retraining.

Important note: In this theoretical manuscript, no deep ensembles were trained, no polynomial chaos models were fitted, and no sensor data were collected. The above definitions specify intended future implementations, not completed empirical work.

When:

U^{epi}_t > U^{epi}_{\max}
\quad \text{or} \quad
U^{ale}_t > U^{ale}_{\max},

the system must not interpret uncertainty as evidence of greater threat. Instead, it must:

\text{increase verification, restrict autonomy, and trigger human review}.

This preserves the central distinction:

\text{Uncertainty} \neq \text{Threat}.

4.3 Temporal Filtering and Tactical Patience

Human perception under stress exhibits temporal myopia: recent events are over-weighted while trend information is under-weighted.

To prevent the system from overreacting to transient threat spikes, the estimated threat vector is passed through a low-pass filter:

\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t = \alpha_t \mathbf{T}_t + (1-\alpha_t)\bar{\mathbf{T}}_{t-1},

where the smoothing coefficient decreases as epistemic uncertainty increases:

\alpha_t = \frac{\alpha_0}{1 + \eta U^{epi}_t}.

Here \alpha_0 \in (0,1] and \eta > 0 are calibration constants.

This formalizes tactical patience: the system becomes less responsive to raw estimates when those estimates are unreliable.

4.4 Observability and Active Sensing

No estimator can function safely without sufficient observability. Let the observability Gramian over a horizon N for the linearized dynamics be \mathcal{O}_N. If \mathcal{O}_N is ill-conditioned or rank-deficient in relevant threat dimensions, then U^{epi}_t will remain high regardless of model quality.

In such cases, the system must:

1. request additional sensor modalities,
2. command active sensing actions,
3. restrict autonomy,
4. require human verification.

This prevents the framework from operating indefinitely under perception blindness.

