5. Safety Set and Control Barrier Functions

5.1 Hard Safety Set

The hard safe set is defined as:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{hard}} =
\left\{
x \in X :
h_{\text{phys}}(x) \ge 0,
h_{\text{env}}(x) \ge 0,
h_{\text{mob}}(x) \ge 0
\right\}.

These are hard constraints:

· h_{\text{phys}} protects physiological integrity;
· h_{\text{env}} protects against environmental hazards;
· h_{\text{mob}} protects against positional and mobility hazards.

If any hard barrier approaches zero, the Safety Governor must prioritize that barrier over all operational objectives.

5.2 Control Barrier Function Condition

In continuous time, assume nominal dynamics:

\dot{x} = f(x, u, w).

For each hard barrier h_i, the system must satisfy:

\dot{h}_i(x, u, w) \ge -\alpha_i h_i(x),
\quad \forall w \in W,

where \alpha_i > 0 is a class \mathcal{K} design parameter.

This condition ensures forward invariance of the safe set: if h_i(x_0) \ge 0, then h_i(x_t) \ge 0 for all future times under the chosen control.

5.3 Priority Hierarchy

Physiological safety is the highest-priority barrier. If the subject’s physiological state deteriorates while resistance increases, the system must not interpret resistance as a reason to maintain or increase force.

Formally, the Safety Governor must satisfy:

\dot{h}_{\text{phys}}(x,u,w) \ge -\alpha_{\text{phys}} h_{\text{phys}}(x)

as a lexicographically primary constraint, before threat-related operational objectives are considered.

This prevents the dangerous feedback loop in which resistance caused by respiratory distress is misclassified as escalating threat.

