6. Action Governor and Safety Governor

The operational controller and the Safety Governor are separate. This follows the Action Governor and Black-Box Simplex paradigms.

6.1 Definitions

Let C_O(x_t) be the operational controller — an advanced, possibly learned policy.

Let C_S(x_t, a_O, m_t) be the Safety Governor.

The final action is:

a_t^{\text{final}} = C_S(x_t, C_O(x_t), m_t).

The Safety Governor does not simply accept or reject. It solves a constrained projection problem:

a^* = \arg\min_{a \in A}
\left\| a - a_O \right\|^2

subject to:

h_i(x,a) \ge 0,
\quad \forall i \in \{\text{phys}, \text{env}, \text{mob}\},

\dot{h}_i(x,a,w) \ge -\alpha_i h_i(x),
\quad \forall w \in W,
\quad \forall i,

\mathcal{F}(a) \le F_{\max}(\Phi, E, M).

If the problem is feasible, the Safety Governor outputs a^*. If the problem is infeasible, the Safety Governor:

1. substitutes the most recent verified backup safe action a^{\text{safe}}_{t-1},
2. or transitions to Fail-Safe Mode.

6.2 Hard vs. Soft Constraints

Constraint Type Examples Override
Hard Physiological barrier, environmental barrier, mobility barrier, hard force envelope Cannot be overridden by human or AI
Soft Operational thresholds, recommended force ceiling, de-escalation timing Can be overridden by human with active justification and logging

This distinction resolves the conflict between envelope collapse and human override. If the hard envelope collapses, no human override is permitted. The system enters Fail-Safe. If only a soft restriction is exceeded, a human may override it through the Cognitive Forcing Function described in Section 9.

