7. Dynamic Safety Envelope and De-escalation

The permissible intervention intensity is bounded by:

0 \le F_t \le F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t),

where:

· \Phi_t is the physiological safety index,
· E_t is the environmental hazard state,
· M_t is the subject mobility and body position.

This bound is hard. No operational controller or human operator may exceed it.

7.1 Minimum Sufficient Intervention

The operational controller must choose the minimum sufficient intervention within this envelope:

a_t^{*} = \arg\min_{a \in A}
\mathcal{F}(a)

subject to:

P_{\text{safe}}(a \mid \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t, \mathbf{U}_t, \Phi_t) \ge 1 - \epsilon,

and:

0 \le \mathcal{F}(a_t) \le F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t).

Here P_{\text{safe}} denotes the estimated probability that action a avoids catastrophic harm under the current state estimate.

7.2 Dynamic De-escalation Property

If the smoothed threat decreases:

\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t < \bar{\mathbf{T}}_{t-1},

then, under normal operating conditions, intervention intensity should not increase:

F_t \le F_{t-1} + \Delta F_{\text{exception}},

where:

\Delta F_{\text{exception}} > 0

only if a hard safety constraint is active and the exception is independently logged.

If no hard safety constraint is active:

\Delta F_{\text{exception}} = 0.

This is a measurable control property, but it is not rigid. A temporary increase in force is permitted only when required by physiological, environmental, or mobility safety constraints, and must be independently auditable.

7.3 Envelope Collapse

If:

F_{\text{required}}(\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t, \mathbf{U}_t) > F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t),

then no autonomous action is permitted. The system transitions to Fail-Safe Mode.

This prevents the system from resolving a conflict between operational necessity and safety constraints by silently overriding safety limits.

---

8. Degradation Modes and Failure Containment

No AI system can guarantee perfect perception. Therefore, the architecture must assume failure.

The system operates in one of three modes:

m_t \in \{0, 1, 2\}.

8.1 Mode 0 — Normal

Entered when:

· U^{epi}_t \le U^{epi}_{\max},
· U^{ale}_t \le U^{ale}_{\max},
· proxy-bias monitor is below threshold,
· sensor degradation is within specified bounds,
· Safety Governor is certified.

In Normal Mode:

· full closed-loop operation is permitted;
· Safety Governor active;
· proxy-bias monitor certified;
· human authority active;
· all hard constraints enforced.

8.2 Mode 1 — Degraded

Entered when:

U^{epi}_t > U^{epi}_{\max}
\quad \text{or} \quad
U^{ale}_t > U^{ale}_{\max},

or when the proxy-bias monitor detects significant feature leakage, or when sensor degradation exceeds threshold.

In Degraded Mode:

· maximum permissible force is reduced by a conservative factor \kappa_{\text{degr}} < 1;
· any force above minimal containment requires dual human confirmation;
· autonomous escalation is disabled;
· verification requirements increase.

8.3 Mode 2 — Fail-Safe

Entered when:

· state estimator fails,
· Safety Governor fails,
· hard envelope collapses,
· no feasible safe action exists.

In Fail-Safe Mode:

· all autonomous force recommendations are disabled;
· the system defaults to minimum necessary containment at distance;
· the primary operational objective becomes protection and summoning backup;
· human authority operates without AI force recommendations.

Transitions between modes must be logged and independently auditable.

