9. Proxy-Bias Detection and Fairness Audit

Identity independence cannot be assumed. It must be continuously tested.

9.1 Proxy-Bias Monitor

A separate Proxy-Bias Detection Layer operates outside the operational force decision path.

This layer uses protected evaluation datasets to estimate:

I(\hat{x}^{op}; g),

the conditional mutual information between operational features and protected identity.

Additionally, an adversarial classifier f_{\text{bias}} is trained to predict g from \hat{x}^{op}. Let its balanced accuracy be B_{\text{acc}}.

If either:

I(\hat{x}^{op}; g) > \delta_I

or:

B_{\text{acc}} > \delta_{\text{acc}},

the system triggers a bias alert.

Consequences include:

· restriction of the operational controller’s autonomy,
· mandatory human review,
· recalibration of the threat-estimation model,
· logging in the protected audit trail.

9.2 Fairness Metrics

Auditing is not limited to direct discrimination. The system must report:

· false positive rates by group,
· false negative rates by group,
· force intensity conditional on true threat,
· outcome disparity under equivalent threat states.

Formal metrics include equalized odds difference:

\Delta_{\text{EO}} =
\max_{y \in \{0,1\}}
\left|
P(\hat{Y}=1 \mid Y=y, G=g_1) -
P(\hat{Y}=1 \mid Y=y, G=g_2)
\right|,

and disparate impact ratio:

\text{DIR} =
\frac{P(F > F_0 \mid G=g_1)}
     {P(F > F_0 \mid G=g_2)}.

The framework requires:

\Delta_{\text{EO}} \le 0.05

and:

0.8 \le \text{DIR} \le 1.25,

where lawful and appropriate. These thresholds are jurisdiction-specific and must be set through governance.

9.3 Feedback-Loop Detection

The monitor also tests for deployment-induced feedback loops.

If the system’s deployment changes:

P(\text{high risk} \mid g)

over time in a manner not explained by underlying incident data, the system must flag potential “predictive policing” dynamics and require governance review.

The distinction remains:

Identity may be audited, but must not determine force.

