10. Human Authority as a Cognitive Forcing Function

Human oversight is not treated as a passive final check. It is redesigned as a Cognitive Forcing Function.

A cognitive forcing function requires the human to perform a deliberate, effortful action before accepting an AI recommendation or overriding a safety restriction.

10.1 Implementation

· The AI may not autonomously authorize force above a calibrated threshold F_{\text{threshold}}.
· To accept a force recommendation above F_{\text{threshold}}, the human must actively confirm and articulate the operational justification.
· To override a soft Safety Governor restriction, the human must state the reason and acknowledge the risk.
· Both actions are logged.

10.2 Judgment Latch

After an escalation in force, the system imposes a minimum reassessment interval:

\Delta t_{\min}.

No further escalation is permitted until:

· the state has been re-estimated,
· the human has completed the required verification,
· the judgment latch has elapsed.

This prevents reflexive escalation chains.

10.3 De-Authorizing the AI Interface

The interface must not present AI recommendations as authoritative.

Recommendations should be framed as:

“One estimate among many.”

Uncertainty bars and safety envelope boundaries should be prominent.

Real-time displays should show:

· uncertainty,
· mode status,
· envelope limits,
· required human verification.

Narrative explanations should not be provided in real time. They increase cognitive load and can reinforce automation bias. Explanations are reserved for post-hoc audit.

