12. Formal Safety Objective and Proposed Safety Invariant

12.1 Revised Objective

Let \rho \in (0,1) be the discount factor. Define the stage cost:

\ell(x_t, a_t) =
w_{\text{cat}} \mathbb{I}_{\text{cat}}(x_t)
+
w_{\text{serious}} \mathbb{I}_{\text{serious}}(x_t)
+
w_{\text{unnecessary}} \mathbb{I}_{\text{unnecessary force}}(a_t)
+
w_{\text{operational}} \mathbb{I}_{\text{operational failure}}(x_t),

where w_{\cdot} are jurisdiction-specific weights set through governance.

The global optimization problem is:

\min_\pi
\mathbb{E}\left[
\sum_{t=0}^{T}
\rho^t
\ell(x_t, a_t)
\right]

subject to:

P(\text{catastrophic harm}) \le \epsilon,

0 \le F_t \le F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t),

h_i(x_t) \ge 0,
\quad \forall i \in \{\text{phys}, \text{env}, \text{mob}\},

U^{epi}_t \le U^{epi}_{\max}
\quad \text{or mode transition},

I(\hat{x}^{op}; g) \le \delta_I,

and the dynamic de-escalation property.

Uncertainty and bias are handled as constraints, not as additive costs. This avoids forcing the optimizer to trade off epistemic uncertainty against physical harm in a single scalar sum.

12.2 Proposed Adaptive Safety Invariant

Version 0.1 called its central statement a theorem. That was premature. Version 0.2 labeled it a proposed design invariant. Version 0.3 stated it as a design invariant requiring formal proof. Version 0.4 presents it as a proposed invariant; no formal proof is supplied in this manuscript.

Proposed Adaptive Safety Invariant (ASI)

Assume:

· A1: In Normal Mode, state-estimation error is bounded:
  \|x_t - \hat{x}_t\| \le e_{\max},
  with probability at least 1 - \delta_{\text{est}}.
· A2: Physiological, environmental, and mobility sensors are operational.
· A3: Safety Governor latency is within the specified budget:
  \tau_{\text{SG}} \le \tau_{\max}.
· A4: The temporal threat filter is stable:
  \alpha_t \in (0,1].
· A5: The proxy-bias monitor is certified and active.
· A6: Human verification cannot be disabled during Normal or Degraded Mode.
· A7: Fail-Safe Mode is available and can be entered within bounded time \tau_{\text{FS}}.

Then, for all t in Normal Mode:

F_t \le F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t).

If the smoothed threat is strictly decreasing:

\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t < \bar{\mathbf{T}}_{t-1},

and no hard safety constraint is active, then:

F_t \le F_{t-1}.

If the system enters the stabilization region:

T_t < T_c
\quad \land \quad
R_t < R_c
\quad \land \quad
M_t < M_c,

and remains there for a dwell time \tau_{\text{stab}}, then there exists a finite time after which:

a_t \in A_{\text{stabilization}}.

The proposed ASI does not claim to eliminate all deaths, injuries, or human error. It establishes a narrower and testable property:

A correctly functioning adaptive intervention controller cannot indefinitely maintain an intervention regime that is formally incompatible with the system’s updated threat, uncertainty, and physiological safety state.

12.3 Proof Strategy

A formal proof of the proposed ASI would require:

· forward invariance proofs for the hard safe set under the CBF conditions,
· reachability analysis for the stabilization region,
· bounded-error propagation from A1,
· induction over discrete time using the judgment latch and mode transitions.

Control barrier function techniques and Lyapunov-barrier arguments would be natural tools for such a proof. However, this manuscript does not provide the proof. It is reserved for future theoretical work.

