13. Legal and Governance Alignment

The proposed system falls within high-risk AI categories under the EU AI Act because it supports law enforcement decisions involving the use of force.

Compliance would require:

· data protection impact assessment,
· fundamental rights impact assessment,
· human oversight by design,
· technical documentation,
· post-market monitoring.

The framework does not use real-time remote biometric identification in public spaces for identity recognition. It also does not use emotion recognition for threat estimation. These design choices reduce the risk of prohibited practices under the EU AI Act.

The following parameters require jurisdiction-specific governance:

\epsilon,\quad
\Phi_{\max},\quad
U_{\max},\quad
F_{\max}(\cdot),\quad
\delta_I,\quad
\Delta t_{\min},\quad
\tau_{\text{stab}},\quad
w_{\cdot}.

Calibration must include:

· participatory community oversight,
· legal review,
· officer and responder input,
· medical and human-factors expertise,
· periodic revalidation,
· public transparency.

No mathematical constant may be imposed as universal without local validation.

