14. Limitations and Absence of Empirical Validation

This manuscript is explicitly theoretical. It does not report:

· experimental results,
· physical tests,
· simulations,
· trained models,
· code,
· deployment data.

This absence is not an oversight. It reflects the current stage of the research: formal specification before empirical evaluation.

The framework has important limitations:

· It is not a legal standard. Operational safety does not equal legal justification.
· It does not eliminate all harm or error.
· It depends on sensor availability and state-estimation quality.
· It does not resolve normative disputes about acceptable risk levels.
· It does not address every institutional, economic, or historical condition affecting use of force.
· The adversarial model is bounded, not exhaustive.
· The formal proof of the proposed Adaptive Safety Invariant remains future work.
· The proxy-bias detection layer assumes availability of protected evaluation datasets; such datasets require careful legal and ethical governance.

Therefore, the claims in this manuscript must be read as architectural and formal, not empirical.

