16. Conclusion

The history of human intervention contains situations in which an action initially intended to restore safety produces catastrophic consequences because the intervention does not adequately adapt to the evolving state of the situation.

The solution should not depend on judging people according to identity.

Nor should it depend entirely on trusting individual judgment.

A more general approach is to engineer the intervention system itself.

The proposed framework treats intervention as a closed-loop control problem under uncertainty. Version 0.4 restates this framework as a purely theoretical contribution: no experiments, no physical tests, no trained models. It defines the system model, the safety constraints, the Safety Governor, the degradation modes, the proxy-bias monitor, the human authority mechanism, and a proposed safety invariant.

Its central proposition remains:

\boxed{
\text{No intervention intensity should become independent of the state that originally justified it.}
}

Threat changes. Therefore intervention must change.

Uncertainty increases. Therefore verification must increase.

Physiological danger increases. Therefore safety constraints must become dominant.

Threat decreases. Therefore force must decrease.

When the system reaches a stable state:

\boxed{
\text{Control must transition into protection.}
}

The ultimate objective is not to replace human judgment with artificial intelligence.

It is to construct human–AI systems in which neither human error nor machine error can easily escape the safety constraints of the architecture.

Such a framework could become a foundation for a broader science of adaptive intervention safety, applicable across societies, institutions, and eventually autonomous civilizations. But that remains a theoretical aspiration until formal proof and empirical validation are achieved.

