Appendix B — Formal State and Threat Definitions

B.1 Full State Vector

For the purposes of the formal architecture, the true state x_t decomposes as:

x_t =
\left(
\mathbf{T}^{\text{true}}_t,
\Phi^{\text{true}}_t,
E^{\text{true}}_t,
M^{\text{true}}_t,
\mathbf{O}_t
\right),

where \mathbf{O}_t represents all other operationally relevant contextual variables, including subject location, available cover, team positions, and communication state.

Only \mathbf{T}_t, \Phi_t, E_t, and M_t enter the force policy and Safety Governor.

B.2 Observable Threat Vector

The threat vector is:

\mathbf{T}_t =
\begin{bmatrix}
T^{\text{weapon}}_t \\
T^{\text{proximity}}_t \\
T^{\text{motion}}_t \\
T^{\text{resistance}}_t \\
T^{\text{environment}}_t
\end{bmatrix}.

Each component is defined as a bounded scalar:

0 \le T^{(j)}_t \le 1,
\quad j \in \{\text{weapon, proximity, motion, resistance, environment}\}.

If a component cannot be reliably estimated, its uncertainty contribution is increased rather than assuming a neutral value.

B.3 Physiological, Environmental, and Mobility Safety States

Physiological state

\Phi_t =
\left(
\Phi^{\text{SpO2}}_t,
\Phi^{\text{HR}}_t,
\Phi^{\text{chest}}_t,
\Phi^{\text{airway}}_t
\right),

where:

· \Phi^{\text{SpO2}}_t is estimated peripheral oxygen saturation;
· \Phi^{\text{HR}}_t is estimated heart rate;
· \Phi^{\text{chest}}_t is estimated chest-wall excursion;
· \Phi^{\text{airway}}_t is an ordinal indicator of airway patency.

Each component is bounded and normalized.

Environmental state

E_t =
\left(
E^{\text{visibility}}_t,
E^{\text{terrain}}_t,
E^{\text{weather}}_t,
E^{\text{hazard}}_t
\right).

Mobility and position state

M_t =
\left(
M^{\text{posture}}_t,
M^{\text{movement}}_t,
M^{\text{restraint}}_t
\right).

The hard force envelope F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t) is a monotone non-increasing function of physiological deterioration, environmental hazard, and positional compromise.

