Appendix C — Uncertainty Quantification Details

C.1 Epistemic Uncertainty via Deep Ensemble

Assume K independently initialized threat-estimation models:

\mathbf{T}_t^{(k)} = g_k(y_{0:t}),
\quad k = 1,\ldots,K.

The ensemble mean is:

\bar{\mathbf{T}}_t =
\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K} \mathbf{T}_t^{(k)}.

The epistemic uncertainty is:

U^{epi}_t =
\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}
\left\|
\mathbf{T}_t^{(k)} - \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t
\right\|^2.

Because the threat vector is normalized, U^{epi}_t \in [0, 1]. Large values indicate model disagreement.

C.2 Aleatoric Uncertainty via Sensor Noise

Let \Sigma_{v,t} be the estimated covariance of observation noise at time t. Aleatoric uncertainty is:

U^{ale}_t = \mathrm{tr}\left(\Sigma_{v,t}\right).

This measures irreducible observation randomness.

C.3 Polynomial Chaos Expansion for Environmental Adaptation

Let \xi denote environmental parameters such as lighting, humidity, temperature, and sensor degradation. A quantity of interest Q_t is expanded as:

Q_t =
\sum_{\alpha}
c_{\alpha}(t)
\Psi_{\alpha}(\xi),

where \{\Psi_{\alpha}\} are orthogonal polynomial chaos bases. Coefficients c_{\alpha}(t) are estimated online.

This allows the perception layer to adapt to environmental variation without full model retraining.

C.4 Uncertainty Thresholds and Mode Transitions

The system enters Degraded Mode if:

U^{epi}_t > U^{epi}_{\max}
\quad \text{or} \quad
U^{ale}_t > U^{ale}_{\max}.

If both thresholds are violated simultaneously, or if state estimation failure is declared, the system may transition directly to Fail-Safe.

