Appendix D — Control Barrier Function Proofs

D.1 Forward Invariance Lemma

Lemma D.1 (CBF forward invariance).
Let h: X \to \mathbb{R} be continuously differentiable. Define:

\mathcal{S} = \{ x \in X : h(x) \ge 0 \}.

Assume the continuous-time dynamics:

\dot{x} = f(x, u, w),

with u \in A, w \in W bounded. If a control policy u = \pi(x) satisfies:

\dot{h}(x, \pi(x), w)
\ge
-\alpha h(x),
\quad
\forall w \in W,

for some \alpha > 0, then \mathcal{S} is forward invariant under \pi.

Proof.
Consider the scalar function y(t) = h(x(t)). The CBF condition implies:

\dot{y}(t) \ge -\alpha y(t).

Define z(t) = y(t) e^{\alpha t}. Then:

\dot{z}(t)
=
\dot{y}(t) e^{\alpha t}
+
\alpha y(t) e^{\alpha t}
=
e^{\alpha t}\left( \dot{y}(t) + \alpha y(t) \right)
\ge 0.

Thus z(t) is non-decreasing. If y(0) \ge 0, then z(0) \ge 0, and hence:

z(t) \ge 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
y(t) e^{\alpha t} \ge 0
\quad \Rightarrow \quad
y(t) \ge 0.

Therefore h(x(t)) \ge 0 for all t \ge 0. \square

D.2 Robust Forward Invariance Under Bounded Disturbance

The same result holds if the inequality is enforced for all w \in W, as stated. The worst-case disturbance is:

w^* = \arg\min_{w \in W} \dot{h}(x,u,w).

If the inequality holds for w^*, it holds for all w.

D.3 Lexicographic Priority Lemma

Lemma D.2 (Physiological priority).
Suppose the safe set is defined by:

\mathcal{S}_{\text{hard}} =
\left\{
x : h_{\text{phys}}(x) \ge 0,
h_{\text{env}}(x) \ge 0,
h_{\text{mob}}(x) \ge 0
\right\}.

If the Safety Governor enforces:

\dot{h}_{\text{phys}}(x,u,w)
\ge
-\alpha_{\text{phys}} h_{\text{phys}}(x)

as a lexicographically primary constraint before all operational objectives, then the system cannot remain in a force regime that violates physiological safety to achieve threat suppression.

Proof sketch.
The lexicographic ordering means the projection problem first restricts A to actions satisfying the physiological CBF condition. Only if the feasible set is nonempty are environmental and mobility constraints considered. If no action satisfies the physiological barrier, the system enters Fail-Safe Mode and disables all autonomous force. Therefore no operational objective can override a physiological violation. \square

