Appendix E — Safety Governor and Action Projection

E.1 Formal Projection Problem

At time t, the Safety Governor receives a_O from the operational controller and solves:

a^* =
\arg\min_{a \in A}
\left\|
a - a_O
\right\|^2

subject to:

h_i(x_t, a) \ge 0,
\quad \forall i \in \{\text{phys, env, mob}\},

\dot{h}_i(x_t, a, w)
\ge
-\alpha_i h_i(x_t),
\quad
\forall w \in W,
\quad \forall i,

\mathcal{F}(a)
\le
F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t).

If the problem is feasible, the output is a^*. If infeasible, the Governor uses the most recent verified backup safe action a^{\text{safe}}_{t-1}, or transitions to Fail-Safe.

E.2 Backup Safe Action

A verified backup safe action is defined as:

a^{\text{safe}} \in A^{\text{safe}}
=
\left\{
a \in A :
h_i(x, a) \ge 0,\ \forall i,
\mathcal{F}(a) \le F_{\text{safe}}
\right\},

where F_{\text{safe}} is a conservative force bound below the hard envelope.

E.3 Safety Governor Hard-Constraint Lemma

Lemma E.1 (Safety Governor preserves hard constraints).
If the projection problem is feasible, then a^* satisfies all hard constraints by construction. If it is infeasible, the backup safe action or Fail-Safe transition ensures no action violating hard constraints is executed.

Proof.
The first statement is immediate from the constraints of the projection problem. The second follows because both fallback choices lie either in A^{\text{safe}} or in the set of actions with no autonomous force. By definition, neither violates hard constraints. \square

---

Appendix F — Dynamic De-escalation Formal Property

F.1 Lemma: No Increase Under Decreasing Filtered Threat

Lemma F.1.
Assume:

1. the system is in Normal Mode;
2. no hard safety constraint is active;
3. the smoothed threat strictly decreases:
   \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t < \bar{\mathbf{T}}_{t-1};
4. the Safety Governor projection problem is feasible.

Then:

F_t \le F_{t-1}.

Proof sketch.
When no hard safety constraint is active, \Delta F_{\text{exception}} = 0. The operational controller is required to minimize force subject to safety and threat constraints. Since the filtered threat has decreased, the minimum sufficient force required to satisfy the threat-conditional safety probability constraint:

P_{\text{safe}}(a \mid \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t, \mathbf{U}_t, \Phi_t)
\ge
1 - \epsilon

cannot require more force than at t-1, because the threat condition is weaker. The projection preserves the controller’s force ordering unless a hard safety constraint binds. Under the assumption that no hard constraint binds, force does not increase. \square

F.2 Exception Logging

If a hard safety constraint becomes active, then:

F_t \le F_{t-1} + \Delta F_{\text{exception}},

with \Delta F_{\text{exception}} > 0. This exception must be logged with:

· the specific hard constraint triggering it,
· the time of onset,
· the force increase magnitude,
· the human verification state.

If no hard constraint is active, \Delta F_{\text{exception}} = 0.

