Appendix G — Degradation Mode Automaton

G.1 Transition Definitions

Let the mode at time t be m_t \in \{0,1,2\}.

Define transition predicates:

P_{\text{degr}} =
\left[
U^{epi}_t > U^{epi}_{\max}
\right]
\vee
\left[
U^{ale}_t > U^{ale}_{\max}
\right]
\vee
\left[
B_{\text{acc}} > \delta_{\text{acc}}
\right]
\vee
\left[
\text{sensor degradation} > \tau_{\text{sens}}
\right].

P_{\text{fail}} =
\left[
\text{estimator failure declared}
\right]
\vee
\left[
\text{Safety Governor failure declared}
\right]
\vee
\left[
\text{projection infeasible and no backup safe action}
\right].

Transition rules:

· If m_t = 0 and P_{\text{degr}}, then m_{t+1} = 1.
· If m_t = 0 and P_{\text{fail}}, then m_{t+1} = 2.
· If m_t = 1 and P_{\text{fail}}, then m_{t+1} = 2.
· If m_t = 1 and not P_{\text{degr}} and not P_{\text{fail}} for a required recovery dwell \tau_{\text{rec}}, then m_{t+1} = 0.
· If m_t = 2, recovery to lower modes requires external reset and recertification.

G.2 Lemma: Mode Transitions Do Not Increase Autonomous Authority

Lemma G.1.
Under the transition rules, autonomous authority is monotonically non-increasing when uncertainty or bias increases.

Proof.
Normal Mode has full closed-loop operation. Degraded Mode reduces maximum force and disables autonomous escalation. Fail-Safe disables all autonomous force. Therefore any transition 0 \to 1 \to 2 reduces autonomy. The reverse transition from Degraded to Normal only occurs after the triggering condition has cleared and a recovery dwell has elapsed, preserving the safety direction. \square

