Appendix I — Cognitive Forcing Function and Judgment Latch

I.1 Formal Model

Let a_O be the operational controller’s recommendation. Let F_{\text{threshold}} be the threshold above which human confirmation is mandatory.

Define the human verification state:

v_t^{\text{human}} \in
\{
\text{not required},
\text{pending},
\text{confirmed with justification},
\text{override soft restriction with justification},
\text{rejected}
\}.

I.2 Authorization Rule

If:

\mathcal{F}(a_O) > F_{\text{threshold}},

then a_O is not executed unless:

v_t^{\text{human}} =
\text{confirmed with justification}.

If a_O would violate a soft Safety Governor restriction, it is not executed unless:

v_t^{\text{human}} =
\text{override soft restriction with justification}.

Hard restrictions cannot be overridden.

I.3 Judgment Latch

After an escalation event at time t_e, the system sets:

\Delta t_{\text{latch}} = \Delta t_{\min}.

No further escalation is permitted until:

t \ge t_e + \Delta t_{\min}

and:

· the state has been re-estimated,
· human verification has been completed.

This rule prevents reflexive escalation chains.

