Appendix J — Audit Log Integrity

J.1 Hash Chain Construction

Each audit entry L_t includes all safety-relevant variables and the previous hash h_{t-1}.

Hash:

h_t = H(L_t \parallel h_{t-1}).

Initial:

h_0 = H(\text{genesis}).

Each entry is signed with Ed25519:

\sigma_t = \text{Sign}_{sk}(h_t).

Periodic Merkle roots:

R_k = \text{MerkleRoot}(h_{i}, \ldots, h_{i+n}),

are anchored to write-once read-many storage.

J.2 Tamper-Evidence Lemma

Lemma J.1 (Tamper evidence).
If any entry L_j, j < t, is modified, then all subsequent hash values h_j, h_{j+1}, \ldots, h_t change, and the Merkle root anchored at WORM storage will not match the recomputed root.

Proof.
By collision resistance of the cryptographic hash function H, changing L_j changes h_j. Since each subsequent hash depends on the previous one, the change propagates forward. The anchored Merkle root is immutable on WORM storage, so tampering is detectable. \square

