Appendix K — Proof Strategy for Proposed Adaptive Safety Invariant

K.1 Main Proposition

The Proposed Adaptive Safety Invariant (ASI) has three clauses:

1. Hard envelope preservation:
   F_t \le F_{\max}(\Phi_t, E_t, M_t).
2. De-escalation under decreasing threat:
   If:
   \bar{\mathbf{T}}_t < \bar{\mathbf{T}}_{t-1}
   and no hard safety constraint is active, then:
   F_t \le F_{t-1}.
3. Stabilization transition:
   If:
   T_t < T_c,\quad R_t < R_c,\quad M_t < M_c
   for a dwell time \tau_{\text{stab}}, then:
   a_t \in A_{\text{stabilization}}.

K.2 Available Lemmas

· Lemma D.1 proves CBF forward invariance.
· Lemma E.1 proves Safety Governor preserves hard constraints.
· Lemma F.1 proves de-escalation under decreasing threat.
· Lemma G.1 proves mode transitions do not increase autonomy.

K.3 Proof Sketch

1. Hard envelope: By Lemma E.1, the Safety Governor permits only actions satisfying F_t \le F_{\max}. Lemma D.1 ensures the hard safe set is forward invariant under the CBF conditions. If no feasible action exists, Fail-Safe Mode disables autonomous force. Therefore clause 1 holds.
2. De-escalation: Under the assumptions of Normal Mode and no active hard constraint, Lemma F.1 applies. Therefore clause 2 holds.
3. Stabilization: Once the state remains in the stabilization region for \tau_{\text{stab}}, the operational objective switches to stabilization. The judgment latch and mode transition logic prevent re-escalation without re-entering a threat condition above threshold. Therefore there exists a finite time after which a_t \in A_{\text{stabilization}}.

The full formal proof requires reachability analysis and formal induction over discrete time. It is reserved for future work.

